# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node Medic — SCOPED passwordless sudo for the medic's app user.
#
# Installs to:  /etc/sudoers.d/010-nodemedic   (mode 0440, root:root)
# REPLACES the blanket:  <user> ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL
#
# THE USER. This file names `nodemedic`, the original medic's account. A CLONE
# runs as `pi`: security/render_sudoers.sh renders the account into the grant
# line, the account's Defaults line and the two rules that carry a user name
# (setfacl u:<user>:rw, usermod dialout <user>) at install time, together with
# the machine's backlight. With the default user the file installs exactly as
# written. Every clone gets this file from its parent's clone flow
# (workflows/clone.py, harden_new_medic).
#
# Every alias below maps to a specific privileged call the Kivy app makes on the
# medic ITSELF (grep-verified against the repo, 2026-07; re-swept 2026-10-08 for
# what a CLONE runs). Commands are pinned to absolute paths (merged-usr
# canonical) and, where safe, to fixed arguments so the user can run ONLY these
# exact operations without a password.
#
# WHICH COMMANDS BELONG HERE — and which deliberately do not:
#   * HERE: what the app runs on the medic itself (original or clone), and what
#     a medic runs on itself while cloning ANOTHER medic (discovery, imaging).
#   * NOT HERE: commands run on a NODE over that node's own connection
#     (workflows/build.py wf.priv, provisioning/link.py, node_sudoers.py,
#     gadget.py, uart_console.py ...) — they meet the NODE's sudoers.
#   * NOT HERE: what a parent sends to a NEW medic during a clone, before the
#     clone's last steps harden it (workflows/clone.py wf.priv / "sudo -n ...")
#     — the new medic still has its card's full sudo at that point.
# tests/test_privileged_commands.py finds every privileged call in the code and
# fails until each one is classified — and, if it runs on the medic, granted
# here. Its sudo matcher follows sudoers(5), not intuition (see WILDCARDS).
#
# WILDCARDS SPAN ARGUMENTS. sudo matches the arguments as ONE string, and `*`
# matches spaces too (sudoers(5), "Wildcards"): `cat /var/log/messages*` also
# allows `cat /var/log/messages /etc/shadow`. So a trailing `*` lets the caller
# append arguments of their own. New rules use exact arguments or single-
# character classes ([a-z], [0-9]); the test lists every rule that still has a
# `*` and why each is harmless — a new one fails until it is reviewed there.
#
# VALIDATE before installing:   visudo -cf provisioning/sudoers.d/nodemedic
# NEVER hand-edit /etc/sudoers.d on the live medic — use the apply script, which
# validates a temp copy with `visudo -c` first (a malformed file locks out sudo).
#
# NOT granted here (deliberately): `bash`, `sh`, `su`, `tee` to arbitrary paths,
# `systemctl` of arbitrary units, editors, package installs beyond gpsd. If the
# app needs a new privileged action, add a NARROW alias — never widen an old one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Screen backlight -------------------------------------------------------
# provisioning/brightness.py  set_brightness() ->
#   sudo -n tee /sys/class/backlight/panel_backlight@1/brightness   (value on stdin)
# tee reads stdin only; the path is fixed, so this can write nothing else.
# panel_backlight@1 is the developer's panel: security/render_sudoers.sh puts
# the installing machine's own /sys/class/backlight device here at install.
Cmnd_Alias NM_BACKLIGHT = \
    /usr/bin/tee /sys/class/backlight/panel_backlight@1/brightness

# --- WiFi (offline-first field networking) ----------------------------------
# provisioning/wifi.py  scan_networks() -> sudo -n iw dev <iface> scan
#                       set_autoconnect() -> sudo -n nmcli connection modify <ssid>
#                         connection.autoconnect <yes|no> [connection.autoconnect-priority <n>]
# sudo matches arguments positionally (a trailing `*` is ONE arg, not "the rest"),
# so both emitted shapes are listed explicitly and pinned to `connection modify`.
Cmnd_Alias NM_WIFI = \
    /usr/sbin/iw dev * scan, \
    /usr/bin/nmcli connection modify * connection.autoconnect *, \
    /usr/bin/nmcli connection modify * connection.autoconnect * connection.autoconnect-priority *

# --- RTNode-2400 birth: hop to the node's setup AP + back (MEDIC-LOCAL) ------
# workflows/rtnode_portal.py (wired in ui/app.py) — NetworkManager blocks wifi
# activation via polkit for the login user, so these need sudo:
#   join/rejoin an AP:  nmcli device wifi rescan ssid <s> ; nmcli device wifi connect <s>
#   read the medic's OWN psk to hand to the built node:
#     nmcli -s -g 802-11-wireless-security.psk connection show <name>
# (Found in review: the original inventory missed this medic-local surface, which
# would have broken RTNode birth under the scoped sudoers.)
Cmnd_Alias NM_PORTAL = \
    /usr/bin/nmcli device wifi rescan ssid *, \
    /usr/bin/nmcli device wifi connect *, \
    /usr/bin/nmcli -s -g 802-11-wireless-security.psk connection show *

# --- Wi-Fi radio on/off (Settings ▸ Wi-Fi switch, keeper 2026-10-06) --------
# provisioning/wifi.py set_radio(): the operator turns the medic's Wi-Fi off
# (an offline clone test, a quiet site) and back on. Exact argv, no wildcard.
Cmnd_Alias NM_RADIO = \
    /usr/bin/nmcli radio wifi off, \
    /usr/bin/nmcli radio wifi on

# --- System clock / timezone ------------------------------------------------
# provisioning/tool_datetime.py  set_datetime()/set_timezone()/sync_from_gps()
# monitor/gps_clock.py           apply_clock()  -> set-ntp false, set-time, and
#                                set-ntp true (rollback if set-time then fails)
Cmnd_Alias NM_CLOCK = \
    /usr/bin/timedatectl set-ntp false, \
    /usr/bin/timedatectl set-ntp true, \
    /usr/bin/timedatectl set-time *, \
    /usr/bin/timedatectl set-timezone *

# --- Service control (only the medic's OWN daemons, restart/stop/poweroff) ---
# provisioning/power.py           power_off()          -> systemctl poweroff
# workflows/node_mode.py          set_mode()           -> systemctl restart rnsd|lxmd
# monitor/self_diagnose_runtime.py restart_splitter    -> systemctl restart rnode-splitter
# workflows/gps_setup.py          _configure_gpsd()    -> systemctl enable|restart gpsd*
# A CLONE's app runs as the kiosk unit reticulum-node-medic.service
# (workflows/clone.py configure_autostart); its keeper restarts the app with
#   sudo -n systemctl restart reticulum-node-medic.service
# The original medic starts its UI another way (scripts/restart_ui.sh) and has
# no unit of that name, so there the line grants nothing.
Cmnd_Alias NM_SERVICES = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl poweroff, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart rnsd, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart lxmd, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart rnode-splitter, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable gpsd.socket gpsd, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart gpsd.socket gpsd, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart reticulum-node-medic.service

# --- USB hub power-cycle (autonomous board recovery during flashing) --------
# workflows/robust_flash.py  find_hub_port() -> sudo -n uhubctl (list)
#                            power_cycle()   -> sudo -n uhubctl -l <hub> -p <port> -a off|on
Cmnd_Alias NM_UHUBCTL = \
    /usr/sbin/uhubctl, \
    /usr/sbin/uhubctl -l * -p * -a *

# --- Card mounts: where, and how (every rule that mounts a card) ------------
# Since 2026-10-08 every card the app mounts goes to a folder under
# /run/nodemedic (provisioning/card_mount.py), never /tmp:
#   * /run is root's, so these folders are root's. The app creates them with
#     ONE exact rule each (`install -d ... /run/nodemedic /run/nodemedic/<x>`,
#     owner root, mode 0755 so the app can still READ a mounted card), and it
#     cannot put anything of its own in their place.
#   * the mount is pinned whole: one option string, a device pattern made of
#     single-character classes (no `*`, which spans — WILDCARDS above), one
#     folder. The options are the same for every card:
#       nosymfollow  a symbolic link stored on the card is never followed
#       nodev        a device file stored on the card opens nothing
#       nosuid, noexec  nothing on the card gains privilege or runs
#     so a file root writes into one of these folders (the tee and touch rules
#     below) lands on the card and nowhere else. nosymfollow needs Linux 5.10
#     and util-linux 2.38: Debian 12 or later, which every medic runs.
#   * tee, touch and umount name exact files and folders.
# The medic's own system disk is mmcblk0 on this build, and no rule names it —
# a medic that boots from something else MUST re-derive the device patterns
# rather than widen them. The Python guards (sd_edit.medic_root_disk,
# pi_imager.is_safe_target) still refuse the medic's own disk; this is the
# layer that holds when Python is bypassed.

# --- Inserted-SD boot editing (bake wired-link reachability) -----------------
# provisioning/sd_edit.py  bake_reachability_via_sd() mounts a USB SD reader's
# boot partition at /run/nodemedic/sd_boot and edits config.txt / cmdline.txt.
# workflows/mitosis_card.py verify_medic_card() reads a new medic's card back
# through the same folder (its boot partition) and through
# /run/nodemedic/piboot below (its root partition).
# USB card readers (/dev/sdX<n>) and a second MMC controller (/dev/mmcblk1p<n>,
# a built-in reader) only.
Cmnd_Alias NM_SD_EDIT = \
    /usr/bin/install -d -m 0755 -o root -g root /run/nodemedic /run/nodemedic/sd_boot, \
    /usr/bin/mount -o nosymfollow\,nodev\,nosuid\,noexec /dev/sd[a-z][0-9] /run/nodemedic/sd_boot, \
    /usr/bin/mount -o nosymfollow\,nodev\,nosuid\,noexec /dev/mmcblk1p[0-9] /run/nodemedic/sd_boot, \
    /usr/bin/umount /run/nodemedic/sd_boot, \
    /usr/bin/tee /run/nodemedic/sd_boot/config.txt, \
    /usr/bin/tee /run/nodemedic/sd_boot/cmdline.txt

# --- On-medic Pi SD imaging (BIRTH of a fresh node's card) ------------------
# provisioning/pi_imager.py  flash() writes Pi OS to a USB card reader.
# is_safe_target() (Python) refuses anything but a present REMOVABLE USB disk;
# it can never be the medic's system disk.
#
# THE DEVICE PATTERNS ARE PINNED TO USB READERS. `dd of=/dev/*` let anyone with
# the app's shell overwrite the medic's own system disk without a password.
# /dev/sd* is what a USB card reader enumerates as; the medic's own disk is
# mmcblk0. A clone that boots from a different device must re-derive these,
# never widen them.
# dd's target is ONE character class, not `*`: a `*` there spans arguments
# (WILDCARDS above) and would let a caller add dd operands of their own
# (2026-10-08). provisioning/pi_imager.py write_image_command() writes the
# whole card, /dev/sdX, exactly in this form.
# /run/nodemedic/piboot is a fresh card's boot partition for pi_imager's
# first-boot seed (custom.toml, ssh), and the new medic's ROOT partition for
# the clone check (workflows/mitosis_card.py); card mounts as described above.
Cmnd_Alias NM_IMAGING = \
    /usr/bin/dd of=/dev/sd[a-z] bs=4M conv=fsync status=progress, \
    /usr/sbin/partprobe /dev/sd*, \
    /usr/bin/install -d -m 0755 -o root -g root /run/nodemedic /run/nodemedic/piboot, \
    /usr/bin/mount -o nosymfollow\,nodev\,nosuid\,noexec /dev/sd[a-z][0-9] /run/nodemedic/piboot, \
    /usr/bin/umount /run/nodemedic/piboot, \
    /usr/bin/tee /run/nodemedic/piboot/custom.toml, \
    /usr/bin/touch /run/nodemedic/piboot/ssh, \
    /usr/bin/sync, \
    /usr/local/lib/nodemedic/prepare-card --device /dev/sd* --config /tmp/*

# --- Pi-as-card-reader (rpiboot pushes the mass-storage payload to a blank Pi)
# provisioning/pi_usbboot.py rpiboot_command() -> sudo -n rpiboot [-d <payload>]
# WHY THESE TWO ENTRIES EXIST AS A PAIR: both were granted by hand on the live
# medic and never written back here, so this template — the one a CLONE gets —
# would have produced a medic that images a card and then fails to configure it,
# and a Pi that never becomes a card reader at all (audit, 2026-08-03).
# BENCH-VERIFY the absolute paths before trusting this: sudo matches on the full
# path, and rpiboot may live in /usr/bin or /usr/local/bin depending on how it
# was installed.
Cmnd_Alias NM_PIBOOT = \
    /usr/bin/rpiboot, \
    /usr/bin/rpiboot -d *, \
    /usr/local/bin/rpiboot, \
    /usr/local/bin/rpiboot -d *

# --- USB-gadget link bring-up (claim host end of the /29 to a plugged node) --
# provisioning/link.py  discover_peer() -> sudo -n ip addr add 10.55.0.2/29 dev <if>
#                                          sudo -n ip link set <if> up
# provisioning/direct_link.py discover_peer() does the same on the clone CABLE,
# and first release_own_peer_address():
#                         sudo -n ip addr del 10.55.0.1/29 dev <if>
# A CLONE holds the cable address 10.55.0.1 for life (its card's
# nodemedic-cable-ip unit). When it clones the NEXT medic it must let go of it,
# or both ends of the cable claim the new medic's address. The original medic
# never holds 10.55.0.1, so there the rule is inert. Only that one address can
# be deleted; `ip` writes no files and runs nothing.
Cmnd_Alias NM_USBLINK = \
    /usr/bin/ip addr add 10.55.0.2/29 dev *, \
    /usr/bin/ip addr del 10.55.0.1/29 dev *, \
    /usr/bin/ip link set * up

# --- The medic's OWN radio set-up (first run on a clone: workflows/medic_radio.py)
# A new medic sets up its own Heltec Wireless Tracker AFTER the clone flow has
# scoped its sudo, so every privileged step of MedicRadioSetup must be here:
#   sudo -n /usr/local/lib/nodemedic/radio-units --port /dev/serial/by-id/<board>
#       the root-owned helper (assets/scripts/radio_units.py, installed by the
#       clone flow) writes rnode-splitter, rnsd, lxmd and the hand-over unit
#       from FIXED text for the calling user, then daemon-reloads. It refuses
#       any other argument list and any port that is not a serial by-id link,
#       so the `*` (which spans) cannot smuggle anything in.
#   sudo -n systemctl enable rnode-splitter.service rnsd.service lxmd.service
#   sudo -n systemctl restart rnode-splitter.service
#   sudo -n systemctl start --no-block nm-radio-handover.service
#   sudo -n systemctl restart rnsd.service lxmd.service   (no kiosk unit)
# The OLD route — `install` a user-written temp file into /etc/systemd/system,
# `sh -c`, `systemd-run sh -c` — can never be argument-pinned (whoever writes a
# unit chooses its User= and ExecStart=), so it is NOT here: it remains only as
# the fallback for a medic that still has full sudo and no helper.
Cmnd_Alias NM_RADIO_SETUP = \
    /usr/local/lib/nodemedic/radio-units --port /dev/serial/by-id/*, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl enable rnode-splitter.service rnsd.service lxmd.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart rnode-splitter.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start --no-block nm-radio-handover.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl restart rnsd.service lxmd.service

# --- GPS (gpsd wiring for the Tracker feeding the medic's clock/position) ----
# workflows/gps_setup.py  _configure_gpsd() writes /etc/default/gpsd + installs gpsd.
# (systemctl enable/restart gpsd* are in NM_SERVICES above.)
Cmnd_Alias NM_GPS = \
    /usr/bin/tee /etc/default/gpsd, \
    /usr/bin/apt-get install -y gpsd gpsd-clients

# --- Diagnostics + auto-fixes on the medic's OWN onboard radio ---------------
# diagnostics/base.py _priv() + system_health.py/power_hardware.py -> sudo -n dmesg
# diagnostics/reticulum_software.py -> sudo -n ss -tlnp  (read-only port check)
#                                      sudo setfacl -m u:<user>:rw <serial port>
#                                      sudo usermod -aG dialout <user>
# setfacl is pinned to SERIAL ports only. The old `/dev/*` also matched block
# devices and /dev/mem — letting a compromised app grant itself rw on the medic's
# own root disk (a full-root escalation, verified in the sandbox).
# 2026-10-08, the same lesson one level down: a TRAILING `*` spans arguments
# (WILDCARDS above), and setfacl, usermod and dmesg all accept more arguments
# than the app passes. So each rule is now exact: the ACL names THIS medic's
# user (rendered at install), the port is a tty name of fixed shape
# (character classes, no `*`), usermod adds THIS user to dialout and nobody
# else, and dmesg is the one level list the diagnostics read. A by-id port
# (/dev/serial/by-id/...) is resolved to its tty by the caller first
# (diagnostics/reticulum_software.py _fix_acl) — setfacl followed the link to
# that tty anyway.
Cmnd_Alias NM_DIAG = \
    /usr/bin/dmesg --level=emerg\,alert\,crit\,err\,warn, \
    /usr/bin/ss -tlnp, \
    /usr/bin/setfacl -m u\:nodemedic\:rw /dev/ttyACM[0-9], \
    /usr/bin/setfacl -m u\:nodemedic\:rw /dev/ttyACM[0-9][0-9], \
    /usr/bin/setfacl -m u\:nodemedic\:rw /dev/ttyUSB[0-9], \
    /usr/bin/setfacl -m u\:nodemedic\:rw /dev/ttyUSB[0-9][0-9], \
    /usr/bin/setfacl -m u\:nodemedic\:rw /dev/ttyAMA[0-9], \
    /usr/bin/setfacl -m u\:nodemedic\:rw /dev/ttyAMA[0-9][0-9], \
    /usr/bin/setfacl -m u\:nodemedic\:rw /dev/ttyS[0-9], \
    /usr/sbin/usermod -aG dialout nodemedic

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A password is never remembered for the app account. The medic's sudo keeps
# one time stamp for all of an account's sessions (timestamp_type=global), so
# a password the keeper types for this account would otherwise hold for every
# process of it for minutes after. With 0, only the exact rules above ever run
# without one. Rendered for the account like the grant line below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Defaults:nodemedic timestamp_timeout=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The single grant. The app user (`nodemedic` here; rendered as `pi` on a
# clone) may run ONLY the above, only as root, no TTY, no password. Anything
# else falls through to the default (denied / requires a password that this
# login has no way to supply).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
nodemedic ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: NM_BACKLIGHT, NM_WIFI, NM_PORTAL, NM_RADIO, NM_CLOCK, \
    NM_SERVICES, NM_UHUBCTL, NM_SD_EDIT, NM_IMAGING, NM_PIBOOT, NM_USBLINK, \
    NM_RADIO_SETUP, NM_GPS, NM_DIAG
